    "['local-address [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 200', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 200\\n neighbor 3.3.3.3 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nThe explanation:\\n- In Cisco, you don\\'t explicitly set a local address per BGP group like Juniper\\n- Instead, you configure the source interface for BGP updates under the neighbor configuration\\n- The Loopback0 interface would need to have the 4.4.4.4 address configured in Cisco (equivalent to Juniper\\'s lo0.0)\\n- The AS number 200 comes from the \"peer-as 200\" in the original Juniper configuration\\n- The neighbor address 3.3.3.3 comes from the original neighbor statement', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 200'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 update-source Loopback0', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 200\\n neighbor 3.3.3.3 update-source Loopback0\\n##\\n\\nThe explanation:\\n- In Cisco, you don\\'t explicitly set a local address per BGP group like Juniper\\n- Instead, you configure the source interface for BGP updates under the neighbor configuration\\n- The Loopback0 interface would need to have the 4.4.4.4 address configured in Cisco (equivalent to Juniper\\'s lo0.0)\\n- The AS number 200 comes from the \"peer-as 200\" in the original Juniper configuration\\n- The neighbor address 3.3.3.3 comes from the original neighbor statement', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 update-source Loopback0'}]]": 1,
    "['family [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'address-family l2vpn evpn', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP EVPN signaling configuration:\\n\\n##\\naddress-family l2vpn evpn\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3 activate\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, the EVPN signaling is configured under the L2VPN EVPN address family\\n2. The \"activate\" command enables EVPN signaling for the specified neighbor\\n3. The neighbor AS and other BGP parameters would be configured separately in the main BGP configuration\\n4. Cisco uses \"l2vpn evpn\" instead of Juniper\\'s simple \"evpn\" family designation\\n\\nThe complete BGP configuration in Cisco would include additional context for the peer group, AS number, and other parameters shown in the original Juniper configuration.', 'trans_template': 'address-family l2vpn evpn'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 activate', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP EVPN signaling configuration:\\n\\n##\\naddress-family l2vpn evpn\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3 activate\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In Cisco, the EVPN signaling is configured under the L2VPN EVPN address family\\n2. The \"activate\" command enables EVPN signaling for the specified neighbor\\n3. The neighbor AS and other BGP parameters would be configured separately in the main BGP configuration\\n4. Cisco uses \"l2vpn evpn\" instead of Juniper\\'s simple \"evpn\" family designation\\n\\nThe complete BGP configuration in Cisco would include additional context for the peer group, AS number, and other parameters shown in the original Juniper configuration.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 activate'}]]": 1,
