    "['family [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'address-family vpnv4 unicast', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\naddress-family vpnv4 unicast\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3 activate\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"inet-vpn\" in Juniper translates to \"vpnv4\" in Cisco\\n2. The \"unicast\" keyword remains the same\\n3. The neighbor activation is required in Cisco when adding address families, while Juniper implicitly activates it when configuring the family under a group', 'trans_template': 'address-family vpnv4 unicast'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 activate', 'parent_command': ['vpdn-group [parameter1]'], 'root': 'vpdn-group [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper BGP configuration:\\n\\n##\\naddress-family vpnv4 unicast\\nneighbor 3.3.3.3 activate\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. \"inet-vpn\" in Juniper translates to \"vpnv4\" in Cisco\\n2. The \"unicast\" keyword remains the same\\n3. The neighbor activation is required in Cisco when adding address families, while Juniper implicitly activates it when configuring the family under a group', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 3.3.3.3 activate'}]]": 1,
